## Quick start

```bash
uv sync
cp .env.example .env
# edit .env — set DJANGO_SECRET_KEY (prod settings reject the placeholder)
uv run python manage.py migrate
uv run python manage.py createsuperuser
```

{% if use_docker %}
### Local (uv)

```bash
uv run python manage.py runserver
```

### Docker (dev)

```bash
docker compose up --build
```

### Docker (prod-oriented)

```bash
uv lock                      # once: commit uv.lock so image builds are reproducible
docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml up --build
```

The production image (the default `docker build .` target) has no dev dependencies, build
tools or `uv`; it runs `collectstatic` at build time and starts Gunicorn from its virtualenv.
Set `DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS` (and a real `DJANGO_SECRET_KEY`) in `.env`: prod settings refuse to
start without them. The dev Compose file builds the `dev` target instead.
{% else %}
```bash
uv run python manage.py runserver
```
{% endif %}

## API docs

With the server running (locally they are open; with `config.settings.prod` they require a
logged-in admin: sign in at `/admin/` first):

- Swagger UI: http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/schema/swagger-ui/
- ReDoc: http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/schema/redoc/
- OpenAPI schema: http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/schema/

## Auth

JWT endpoints:

- `POST /api/auth/token/`
- `POST /api/auth/token/refresh/`

These endpoints are rate limited (`DEFAULT_THROTTLE_RATES["auth"]`, default 10/min per client).
Behind a reverse proxy set `DJANGO_NUM_PROXIES` to the number of proxies so clients are told
apart by their real address.

{% if use_multitenancy %}
## Multitenancy

Tenant-scoped models inherit `TenantAwareModel` from `apps.core`.
Send header `X-Tenant-ID: <tenant-uuid>` on API requests.
Writes without a resolved tenant fail closed; reads return empty querysets.
{% endif %}

## Access

By default every authenticated user can read and change **every record** of the generated models{% if use_multitenancy %} (within their tenant){% endif %}, and the API docs are admin-only in production. Add ownership or role rules before exposing real data: see "Who can access what" in the django-octave docs (Concepts → Security). Note that the `owner` permission preset protects single objects, not list endpoints.

Run `uv lock` and `uv audit` to pin and check dependencies.

